</p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"color:black">For high resolution pict=
ures, we started to implement the IMX477 camera, making us switch from a 50=
 mm focal length achromatic doublet to a 125 mm mm focal length achromatic =
doublet (both 12.7 mm in diameter).<o:p></o:p></span></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"color:black"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span>=
</p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"color:black">My job will soon be to r=
efactor the affected parts to use most of the resources of the given camera=
. Now, the team I am working with uses AutoCAD occasionally but mostly Soli=
d Edge for model manipulation. I am
 a rookie to this, but as far as my understanding is correct, scaling a par=
t up is just done by setting the according parameters - hard to build, but =
easy to replicate.<o:p></o:p></span></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"color:black"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span>=
</p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"color:black">I will go down the road =
to deliver the message, that if something is already perfectly built, nothi=
ng needs to change, but do you happen to know any reliable way to provide t=
hose guys with some PAR files to play
 with?<o:p></o:p></span></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"color:black"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span>=
</p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"color:black">I cannot be the only one=
, who this is happening to. Why must it be so hard, when it can come so eas=
y?<o:p></o:p></span></p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"color:black"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></span>=
</p>
</div>
<div>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><span style=3D"color:black">Alright, father Julian. =
Thank you for your attention. I hope this made your day.<o:p></o:p></span><=
/p>
